  "final": "`stream_rows` and `take` now close the resource exactly once in each case you listed. `python3 checks/public.py` passes, including the checks I added. I didn't test the cleanup-raises cases, such as `close()` failing during an iteration error.\n\n**`src/service.py`, `stream_rows`:**\n- On exhaustion it closes the resource once. If that close raises, the error propagates.\n- On an explicit `generator.close()` it closes once and re-raises `GeneratorExit`. A close error propagates.\n- On a read error or a transform error it closes once and re-raises the original error. A second error from the close is swallowed. After a transform failure no further row is read.\n- An unstarted generator does no read or close, and closing it does nothing. It is still lazy and keeps row order and transformed values.\n\n**`src/consumer.py`, `take`:**\n- A limit of zero or less returns `[]` without creating or starting the stream. The spec only covers zero and positive limits, so treating negatives the same way is my choice.\n- Otherwise it stops after `limit` rows and closes the stream.\n- If consuming fails, it closes the stream and re-raises the original error. A close error during that cleanup is swallowed.\n\n**Checks added to `checks/public.py`:**\n- Closing an unstarted generator does no read or close.\n- Calling `close()` twice on a started generator closes once.\n- A transform error closes once and propagates.\n- `take` with limit 2 reads 2 rows and closes once.\n- `take` with limit 0 does no read and no close.",
